WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.000
Every time you update a React Native app, you have to open the simulator and manually tap through every screen to make sure your changes didn't break a core feature.

2
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.000
Over time, use manual checks become a massive drain on development speed.

3
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Detox addresses this by providing an end-to-end testing framework.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:25.000
It controls a real iOS simulator, simulating swipes, taps and text input exactly as a user would.

5
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:33.000
In this guide, we will install Detox, configure it for an iOS environment, and write a script that automates a standard login flow.

6
00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:40.000
To follow along, you'll need a macOS environment with Xcode installed, an existing React Native project, and the homebrew package manager.

7
00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Once these tools are in place, we can begin moving away from manual testing toward a consistent automated pipeline.

8
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:52.000
First, install the Detox command line tools and library into your project using a package manager.

9
00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.000
We also need two OS-level utilities.

10
00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:03.000
Use homebrew to install Apple Simutils and XC pretty in your terminal. Apple Simutils is on a wire meant for this workflow.

11
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:10.000
It acts as the communication layer that allows the Detox test runner to send instructions directly to the iOS simulator.

12
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Now run the initialization command to create a dedicated E2E folder and generate the base configuration files. Open the new configuration file.

13
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:25.000
You must replace these placeholder strings with the actual workspace and scheme names defined in your Xcode project.

14
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:33.000
Next, check the binary path in the same file. This tells Detox exactly where to find the app file generated during the build process.

15
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Finally, adjust the just settings to avoid a common failure point during execution.

16
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.000
As this diagram shows, trying to run multiple tests at once on a singular simulator causes the runner to hang.

17
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:51.000
To prevent this, you must set the max workers property to one, ensuring only one test file runs at a time.

18
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:57.000
These steps ensure the runner can successfully find and control your specific application.

19
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:02.000
Before writing the test, we must make our UI elements visible to the runner.

20
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:11.000
By default, the test runner has no way to distinguish one button from another. We solve this by adding a test ID prop to our react native components.

21
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:20.000
On iOS, this maps directly to the native accessibility identifier. Use stable descriptive IDs rather than dynamic list indexes.

22
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:28.000
This ensures the test still works even if the item order changes. With the UI tagged, we move back to the terminal to compile the app.

23
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:35.000
Run the Detox build command. This triggers a native compilation specifically optimized for the iOS simulator.

24
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:45.000
This step takes a few minutes as it performs a full Xcode build of your project. Note that you only need to run this full build when you change native code.

25
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.000
If you only modify JavaScript, Detox reloads the bundle instantly without a full rebuild.

26
00:02:50.000 --> 00:02:55.000
Once the build finishes, you have a specialized testing binary ready for automation.

27
00:02:55.000 --> 00:03:00.000
Open the sample test file in the E2E folder so we can script our login sequence.

28
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:13.000
We start by telling the runner to find the email input field using our test ID. By using the type text action, the runner will simulate the physical interaction of a user entering an email address into the field.

29
00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:17.000
Next, we add a command to locate the login button and perform a tap.

30
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:25.000
To finish the script, we add an expectation. This tells the test to wait until the success screen is visible before passing.

31
00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:34.000
Now, run the test command. The simulator automatically boots, installs the build, and navigates through the entire login screen sequence on its own.

32
00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:42.000
When the sequence finishes, check your terminal for the results. A green success message confirms the login flow is functioning correctly in the current build.

33
00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:47.000
You've just successfully automated a native interaction on the iOS simulator.

34
00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:56.000
By configuring your environment, tagging your UI, and scripting the user path, you've created a test that can be repeated as many times as needed.

35
00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:06.000
To scale this, you can integrate these tests into a continuous integration pipeline using macOS runners, ensuring your app is tested before every pull request is merged.

36
00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:16.000
You can even use Detox to pre-grant system permissions like location or camera access, allowing you to test complex workflows without manual interruptions.

37
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:26.000
Every automated test you add reduces the need for repetitive manual checks, giving you more time to focus on developing new features.

